@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+The static page loads `daily-grind.mjs`; no build is required. Run the validation
+tests with `npm test` (Node.js). They use no browser or server.
+
+Configure `TRUSTED_ORDERING_ORIGIN` and `TRUSTED_SITE_DOMAIN` in
+`origin-validation.mjs` for your deployment. The defaults assume the ordering
+service is `https://orders.dailygrind.coffee` and that you control the private
+registrable domain `dailygrind.coffee`. Never configure a public suffix such as
+`co.uk` or `github.io` as the site domain.
+
+Ordering messages must come from that exact origin (scheme, hostname and port).
+Supported payloads are `{ type: 'order:status', message: '...' }` and
+`{ type: 'order:redirect', url: '...' }`. Redirects permit HTTP(S) URLs on the
+page's schemeful site, reject credentials, and resolve relative paths against
+the page URL. Status text is limited to 300 characters and rendered as text.
+Web links are classified as internal or external; external links receive
+`external noopener noreferrer`, and unsafe link schemes are blocked on activation.
+
+The rewards iframe uses `sandbox="allow-scripts"`, without `allow-same-origin`.
+Only an `init` message from its captured window can pin the sender. With native
+`Origin` support, its opaque origin is pinned once and checked on every message,
+including after navigation. No other iframe can initialize or replace it.
+
+Without `Origin`, URL parsing still enforces exact ordering origins. Same-site
+checks use the explicitly configured private domain; on other deployments they
+conservatively accept only the page's exact origin. Widget checks pin the known
+window instead: browsers without `Origin` cannot distinguish opaque documents
+across navigation within that same window. The native-API unit tests use a
+contract double; they do not verify a browser's implementation or suffix list.
